## Solution

Let $z = x + iy$.

**Second condition.** We have $z \neq 0$ and
$$\left|\tfrac{1}{z} - 1\right| = \left|\tfrac{1-z}{z}\right| = \frac{|z-1|}{|z|} < 1,$$
which is equivalent to $|z-1| < |z|$ (valid since $|z|>0$). Squaring both sides:
$$(x-1)^2 + y^2 < x^2 + y^2 \;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\; -2x+1 < 0 \;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\; x > \tfrac{1}{2}.$$

**First condition.** $|z-1|<1$ is the open disk of radius $1$ centered at $(1,0)$:
$$(x-1)^2 + y^2 < 1.$$

**The region** is the intersection of the unit disk centered at $(1,0)$ with the half-plane $x > \tfrac{1}{2}$.

The line $x = \tfrac{1}{2}$ is at distance $d = \tfrac{1}{2}$ from the center $(1,0)$. Since $x > \tfrac{1}{2}$ contains the center, the desired region is the **major** circular segment of the unit disk.

The area of the minor segment (cut off by a chord at distance $d$ from the center of a unit circle) is:
$$A_{\text{minor}} = \arccos(d) - d\sqrt{1-d^2}.$$
With $d = \tfrac{1}{2}$:
$$A_{\text{minor}} = \arccos\!\left(\tfrac{1}{2}\right) - \tfrac{1}{2}\sqrt{\tfrac{3}{4}} = \frac{\pi}{3} - \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}.$$

The major segment area is:
$$A = \pi - A_{\text{minor}} = \pi - \frac{\pi}{3} + \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} = \frac{2\pi}{3} + \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}.$$

(The boundary has measure zero, so open vs. closed inequalities do not affect the area.)

$$\boxed{\dfrac{2\pi}{3} + \dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{4}}$$

### PROOF COMPLETE
